---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Screenshot from '../components/Screenshot.astro';
import { REPO_URL } from '../site';
---

<Base
  fullTitle="Email client with fast search across all accounts · OpenWren"
  description="OpenWren indexes every message on your computer, inside its encrypted database. One search box covers all accounts and years: about 30 ms on 200,000 messages."
>
  <section class="page-intro">
    <h1>An email client whose search finds everything, fast</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Search is where most mail programs let you down: a message you can see in the list does not come up, a search
      takes ten seconds, or there are three search boxes that each look somewhere else. OpenWren has one search box.
      It covers every account and every year of mail, and it answers in milliseconds.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button" href="/download/">Download OpenWren</a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="/benchmarks/">See the benchmarks</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="pair">
    <div class="page-body">
      <h2>One box, every account, every year</h2>
      <p>
        Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd> and type. OpenWren searches the sender, the recipients, the subject and the
        text of every message it has synced, in all your accounts at once. Results are listed newest first and grouped
        by year, with the words you searched for highlighted, and each result shows its folder and account.
      </p>
      <p>
        A single word also matches longer words that start with it, so <code>invoic</code> finds "invoice" and
        "invoices". Put several words in quotes to find them as an exact phrase.
      </p>
      <h2>Narrow it down</h2>
      <p>
        Buttons above the results limit the search to one account, to mail with attachments, or to a period: the past
        year, older than one or two years, or dates you choose. You can type the same things:
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>To find</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>from:anna</code></td><td>Mail from a sender</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>to:</code></td><td>Mail sent to a recipient</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>subject:</code></td><td>Words in the subject</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>has:attachment</code></td><td>Mail with attachments</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>is:unread</code>, <code>is:flagged</code></td><td>Unread or flagged mail</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>before:2024-01-31</code>, <code>after:</code></td><td>Mail before or after a date</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>in:archive</code></td><td>Mail in one folder</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Screenshot src="search.png" alt="Searching for &quot;invoice&quot; across three accounts, with filters for account, attachment and date, and results grouped by year" label="Search" width={340} frame={[680, 1240]} />
  </section>

  <section class="page-body">
    <h2>Why it is fast</h2>
    <p>
      OpenWren does not ask your mail server each time you search, and it does not depend on the search index of your
      operating system. It keeps its own full-text index (SQLite FTS5) on your computer and updates it as mail
      arrives. That is why search also works without a connection, and why it does not slow down when the server is
      busy.
    </p>
    <dl class="figures">
      <div><dt>30 ms</dt><dd>A word found in a few hundred of 200,000 messages</dd></div>
      <div><dt>7 ms</dt><dd>The same search on 50,000 messages</dd></div>
      <div><dt>190 ms</dt><dd>The slowest case: a word found in most of 200,000 messages</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p class="muted">
      Median times of the database work, measured on a laptop with an encrypted mailbox; drawing the results comes on
      top. The <a href="/benchmarks/">benchmarks page</a> has the machine, the method and the commands to repeat them.
    </p>

    <h2>The index is encrypted with your mail</h2>
    <p>
      A search index contains the words of all your mail. Many fast search tools keep it as an ordinary file next to
      the mail. In OpenWren the index is a table inside the same encrypted database (SQLCipher) as the messages, so
      there is no readable copy of your mail's text on the disk. The key is kept in your system's keyring. The
      <a href="/security/">security page</a> says more.
    </p>

    <h2>Mail that is not downloaded yet</h2>
    <p>
      When you add an account, OpenWren first fetches the sender, recipients and subject of every message, so all of
      your mail can be found by those within seconds or minutes. The text of the messages follows in the background,
      newest first. Until a message's text has arrived, "Search the server for more" asks your mail server to look
      through it.
    </p>

    <h2>What search does not do yet</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Results are ordered by date, newest first, not by how well they match.</li>
      <li>Words must be spelt as they are in the mail; a typing mistake is not corrected.</li>
      <li>The contents of attachments are not searched, only whether a message has one.</li>
      <li>
        Text in Chinese, Japanese and Korean is not split into words, so searching inside a sentence in those
        languages often finds nothing. This is <a href={`${REPO_URL}/issues/3`}>being worked on</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Try it on your own mail</h2>
    <p>
      OpenWren is free and open source, and works with Gmail and any IMAP account. Microsoft 365 and Outlook.com
      accounts are not supported yet.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button" href="/download/">Download for Windows, Mac or Linux</a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="/docs/#search">Search in the docs</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .pair {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(280px, 1fr) minmax(220px, 340px);
    gap: 24px 48px;
    align-items: start;
    margin-top: 16px;
  }
  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .pair {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
